mk("Oq5TCAKw64Y","How to make an ambient space pad in any synth.  Sound Design Tutorial 1",
 "eno-space-pad-sines-0-12-19-st-triangle-sub-cross-modulated-smooth-random-free-lfos-mixed-per-target-modulated-fx-mix",
 "Planetarium / Eno-style ambient space pad on any synth: sines at 0, +12 and +19 semitones, a triangle sub, two slow free-running smooth-random LFOs that modulate each other's rate and are mixed on every target, low-sustain amp envelope, gentle filter, and LFO-moved reverb/delay mix",
 "Build the classic slow 'space / planetarium / Brian Eno' meditation pad from an init patch on any synth with three oscillators, two LFOs and a reverb and delay. Everything moves a little, and no two parameters move together, so the ear never locks onto a pattern. Pitch stays steady: all movement is in level, timbre and effect mix.",
 ["Any synth (VST, hardware or Web Audio) with at least three oscillators plus a sub/noise source, two or three LFOs with free-run and smooth-random shapes, a low-pass/band-pass filter, reverb and delay","Basic idea of LFO depth and bipolar/unipolar modulation"],
 [("root","string","Note or key to play the pad in (white-key improvisation works)",True),("background_noise","string","Optional consistent natural bed (rain, wind, forest, spaceship hum) mixed very low",False)],
 [("space_pad_patch","synth-preset","Saved subtle evolving pad patch"),("pad_audio","audio","Long held-chord render with slow, non-repeating movement")],
 ["Keep all modulation subtle. If the listener notices a movement, it is too much.",
  "Do not use LFO-driven big filter sweeps on the pad. If you want a sweep, put it on a send/aux filter and use it sparingly.",
  "Steady pitch (Matt's taste): do not modulate oscillator pitch or delay time, except at most a tiny fine-time amount. Keep unison detune motion slight.",
  "Keep delay stereo spread low on pads (save wide ping-pong for sharp arps or percussion).",
  "Keep any background noise bed consistent and natural (rain and birdsong together sounds wrong).",
  PS, A],
 [("Init the patch and set the oscillators to sine waves: osc 1 at 0 st, osc 2 at +12 st (one octave), osc 3 at +19 st (an octave and a fifth).","synth","Organ-like stack; the +19 adds a faint fifth on top"),
  ("Set up two LFOs: rate in Hz (not tempo-synced) and very slow, smooth-random shape (random but gliding, not stepped), free-running so they do not restart on each key, bipolar.","synth LFO","Slow wandering control signals that keep going across notes"),
  ("Cross-modulate the LFOs: send a small amount of LFO 3 to the rate of LFO 2, and of LFO 2 to the rate of LFO 3.","mod matrix","The two LFOs never move in lockstep"),
  ("Modulate the level of the +12 osc with LFO 3 and the +19 osc with LFO 2, plus a smaller amount on osc 1 (the root should move least). On every target, mix a bit of both LFOs, sometimes inverted.","mod matrix","Upper partials fade gently in and out, like a slow chorus"),
  ("Add a sub oscillator one octave down using a triangle wave (square is too buzzy with sines). Add a trace of noise (pink, white or blue) for high-end character, barely audible.","synth","Body underneath and faint air on top, no buzz"),
  ("Amp envelope: long attack, long decay, sustain lowered (sloping down), long release.","amp env","Overlapping notes die back instead of piling up into mud"),
  ("Low-pass filter for roundness with low resonance. Move cutoff only slightly with the LFOs.","filter","Slightly rounder tone; no audible sweep or resonant peak"),
  ("Effects: a big cathedral-type reverb and a delay. Put both LFOs, mixed, on reverb dry/wet, delay dry/wet and delay feedback, but not on delay time. Low-cut the effects returns to avoid mud.","reverb/delay","The space breathes in and out; low end stays clean"),
  ("Turn on unison on the oscillators (several voices) with only slight LFO movement, then stop adding things. Save the patch, take a 5-15 minute break and come back for small balance tweaks.","synth","Choir-like thickness; the patch is done before it gets overloaded"),
  ("Optional: add shimmer reverb tuned to +7 st (a fifth) instead of the default octave, with its mix moved slowly in and out.","shimmer","Soft shimmer that does not get grating when notes are held"),
  ("Optional: add a low-level consistent noise bed (rain, metallic flow, forest) through a slow, subtle band-pass in parallel with the main filter. Add slow LFO on overall volume and pan, plus small EQ dips on any resonant buildup (not many narrow cuts, which cause a comb-filter effect).","noise engine / EQ","Context bed under the pad without screeching resonances")],
 ["No two modulated parameters rise and fall together over a 60 s listen.","Pitch is steady: no audible vibrato or detune wobble beyond light unison.","Holding overlapping chords for 30 s does not build up low-mid mud (sustain is below max and FX returns are low-cut).","Reverb and delay mix audibly breathe over about 20-60 s cycles."],
 ["pad","ambient","space-pad","lfo","smooth-random","free-running","shimmer","meditation","steady-pitch"])

mk("0Jj5qQQzDLU","How to Compose Cinematic Music - FAST",
 "three-track-cinematic-sketch-lp-filter-close-open-reverb-swell-on-runs-rhythmic-autofilter-mix-tempo-ramp-contrary-motion",
 "Fast cinematic sketch with only three tracks (piano, full-orchestra patch, synthetic rhythmic swell layer): contrary-motion second melody, transitions made only by automation (low-pass close then open with reverb-mix swells on runs, rhythmic auto-filter dry/wet rising into the chorus) and a tempo ramp of about 95 to 100 to 115 BPM",
 "Write and arrange a short cinematic cue fast by limiting yourself to three tracks, so you finish the melody, harmony and form before choosing sounds. Without a separate FX track, every section change is made with automation on the existing tracks. This adds the 'three tracks, no FX track' limit and the tempo ramp to the section transition toolkit.",
 ["A DAW with automation lanes and tempo automation","A piano, one full-ensemble orchestral patch (or a layered string/brass synth patch), and a synthetic swell/pulse layer","A big reverb, a low-pass filter and a rhythmic auto-filter/gate effect"],
 [("chords","list","Two-chord emotional core plus variations",True),("tempo_plan","object","Start, steady and climax BPM (about 95, 100, 115 in the video)",False)],
 [("cue_sketch","daw-session","Intro, build run, main chorus and outro on three tracks"),("automation_map","list","Filter, reverb-mix, auto-filter mix and tempo lanes")],
 ["Library and plugin names in the video (piano, orchestra and swell libraries, an auto-filter plugin) are mapped to generic piano, ensemble, swell, filter and reverb modules.",
  "Match drums to Matt's taste: if the cue has a pulse, bring percussion hits in early (within about 8 bars), not only at the chorus.",
  "Tempo ramps only work when everything is MIDI or synth-generated; bounce audio stems after the ramp is final.", A],
 [("Track 1, piano: find two chords that feel emotional going back and forth, add variations, a high ostinato figure on top and a big roll-up. Light low/high EQ lift and a big reverb, then a compressor after the reverb to bring the tail forward.","piano + reverb + comp","Full piano bed that carries harmony and motion alone"),
  ("Track 2, full orchestra in one patch: bass instruments play the chord roots. Write a second melody that answers the piano, moving root to third to fifth, and in contrary motion (when the piano goes up, it goes down). Ride expression (CC) for dynamics.","ensemble patch","Clear two-voice texture; voices separate by direction"),
  ("End the build with a big string run into the main chorus. In the chorus, the brass/strings carry the main melody with high-woodwind sparkle in the same patch. Voice the sections separately only after the arrangement is done.","ensemble patch","Arrangement finished quickly without breaking flow"),
  ("Track 3, synthetic swell layer for rhythm and texture: tweak attack/ADSR, then put a rhythmic auto-filter/envelope effect on it and automate its dry/wet, low in the intro and full in the chorus.","swell synth + auto-filter","The pulse is clearly stronger in the chorus than in the intro"),
  ("Transitions without an FX track: on the piano run and the orchestra before each new section, automate the low-pass filter closing, then opening as the run lands. Automate reverb mix up during the run for a denser wash. Repeat a smaller close/open later for variation.","filter + reverb automation","Sections 'open up' at the downbeat; presence drops then returns"),
  ("Tempo automation: start at about 95 BPM, settle at 100, then ramp to about 110-115 BPM during the last run into the main chorus. Keep the new tempo or ease back for the end section.","tempo lane","The lift into the chorus feels urgent without adding parts"),
  ("Chorus extras: bring in cinematic drum patterns (in the orchestra patch's percussion layer) and have the synth double the bass roots plus the main melody. Outro mirrors the build: synth goes back to roots only.","arrangement","Chorus is the densest point; outro thins out symmetrically"),
  ("Master: narrow the lows to mono, EQ plus compression on lows and mids with gentle upward compression on highs for clarity, light bus compression, final EQ and limiter.","master chain","Mono-safe low end, clear top, controlled peaks")],
 ["The session uses exactly three instrument tracks and no separate FX/riser track.","Each section boundary has a visible filter-close then open automation, and the reverb-mix rises on the run.","The tempo map shows a ramp of at least about 10 BPM into the main chorus.","The orchestra counter-line moves opposite to the piano melody at least at the main phrase peaks."],
 ["arrangement","cinematic","transitions","automation","tempo-ramp","song-structure","contrary-motion","constraint-workflow"])
